# Build output -- ANCHORED ON PURPOSE. See issue #33.
#
# These used to be written bare (`bin/`, `build/`, `dist/`), which in gitignore
# syntax matches a directory of that name at ANY depth. A folder called `build`
# or `bin` inside an imported art pack would then vanish from git entirely --
# not deleted, not conflicted, just absent, and not even listed by
# `git status --ignored`. That is a silent content loss, and the leading slash
# is the whole fix: it pins each pattern to this directory and nowhere else.
#
# A subproject that produces build output of its own carries its own ignore
# file, the way client/.gitignore and tools/hotreload-probe/.gitignore do. That
# keeps every such rule inside the territory it is true for.
/bin/
/obj/
/target/
/build/
/dist/

# Agent worktrees. `git worktree add` puts a COMPLETE second checkout here,
# Library folder and all -- hundreds of megabytes of engine cache that a single
# `git add -A` at the root would otherwise happily commit. Ignoring them makes
# that impossible rather than merely unlikely.
/.claude/worktrees/
/.claude/settings.local.json

# Output of tools/run-unity-tests.ps1, one report per test platform. Ignored so
# the runner's own tree-dirtiness check does not trip over the runner's own
# artefacts. (The .log files beside them are already covered by `*.log`.)
/playmode-results.xml
/editmode-results.xml

# Spike render output — regenerate with ScrubStripCapture
/scrub-strip/
/client/fbx-clip-report.md
